---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/layout/Header.astro';
import MobileMenu from '../../components/layout/MobileMenu.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/sections/PageHero.astro';
import Divider from '../../components/ui/Divider.astro';
import Contact from '../../components/sections/Contact.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema, absUrl, BUSINESS_ID } from '../../lib/schema';

const path = '/insights/coos-county-website-audit';
const published = '2026-06-05';
const headline = 'I audited 169 Coos County websites. Here is what I found.';

const breadcrumbLd = breadcrumbSchema([
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Insights', path: '/insights' },
  { name: 'The Coos County website audit', path },
]);
const articleLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline,
  description:
    'An audit of 169 Coos County small-business websites with Google Lighthouse and Mozilla Observatory. Not one was both fast and secure.',
  url: absUrl(path),
  datePublished: published,
  dateModified: published,
  author: { '@id': BUSINESS_ID },
  publisher: { '@id': BUSINESS_ID },
  image: absUrl('/insights/coos-county-audit-1200.webp'),
  mainEntityOfPage: absUrl(path),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Coos County Website Audit · Winters Code"
  description="I audited 169 Coos County small-business websites with Lighthouse and Mozilla Observatory. Average mobile speed: 61/100. Not one was both fast and secure."
  canonicalPath={path}
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbLd)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleLd)} />
  </Fragment>

  <Header />
  <MobileMenu />

  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Insights · Field Report"
    h1="169 Coos County websites, measured."
    sub="I ran every small-business site I could find through Google Lighthouse and Mozilla Observatory. The numbers are rough, and that is the opportunity."
    bgImage="/photo/local/scenes/sunset-stacks"
    showcase
    bgPosition="center 45%"
  />

  <Divider label="01" />

  <article class="ins">
    <p class="ins-meta mono">Field report · June 2026 · 169 sites audited</p>

    <p class="ins-lead">
      I wanted a real picture of how small-business websites on the south coast
      actually perform, so I measured them. I ran <strong>169 Coos County
      small-business websites</strong> through Google Lighthouse, which scores
      speed, accessibility, best practices, and SEO, and Mozilla Observatory,
      which grades security. Here is what came back.
    </p>

    <figure class="ins-fig">
      <img
        src="/insights/coos-county-audit-800.webp"
        srcset="/insights/coos-county-audit-800.webp 800w, /insights/coos-county-audit-1200.webp 1200w"
        sizes="(min-width: 740px) 640px, 100vw"
        alt="Field report: of 169 Coos County sites audited, zero scored both fast and secure. Average mobile speed 61 of 100, 36 percent had HTTPS with no defenses, and 2 percent had a working sitemap."
        width="1200" height="2133" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Of 169 audited sites, not one scored both fast and secure.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2 class="ins-h">The headline number: zero</h2>
    <p>
      Across all 169 sites, <strong>not a single one scored both fast and
      secure.</strong> Not one. Speed and security are the two things that are
      both measurable and fixable, and every business in the sample was missing
      at least one.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ins-h">Speed: an average of 61 out of 100</h2>
    <p>
      The average Lighthouse mobile speed score was <strong>61 out of
      100</strong>. On the phone, where most local searches actually happen,
      that is the difference between a page that feels instant and one a visitor
      abandons before it loads. Google treats speed as a ranking signal, so slow
      sites lose twice: once in the rankings, and again with the people who do
      not wait.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ins-h">Security: a B at best</h2>
    <p>
      On Mozilla Observatory, <strong>the highest security grade in the entire
      sample was a B, and not one site earned an A.</strong> Thirty-six percent
      had HTTPS, the basic lock icon, but nothing behind it: no real headers, no
      content security policy, no defense in depth. A lock on the door is not the
      same as locking it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ins-h">SEO basics: 2 percent</h2>
    <p>
      Only <strong>2 percent</strong> of the sites had a working sitemap, the
      simple file that tells search engines what pages exist. Most were leaving
      the easiest SEO win on the table.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ins-h">Why: 88 percent are renting</h2>
    <p>
      The reason is structural. <strong>88 percent of the sites were rented from
      a template platform</strong> rather than built. WordPress, Squarespace, and
      Wix made up the bulk of the sample, and the pattern in their scores is hard
      to miss.
    </p>

    <figure class="ins-fig">
      <img
        src="/insights/rental-platforms-800.webp"
        srcset="/insights/rental-platforms-800.webp 800w, /insights/rental-platforms-1200.webp 1200w"
        sizes="(min-width: 740px) 640px, 100vw"
        alt="The three platforms most local businesses rent from: WordPress 63 sites at grade D, Squarespace 13 sites at grade F, Wix 10 sites at grade C, versus custom code averaging 94 on mobile with an A-plus security grade."
        width="1200" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Custom code versus the three platforms most local businesses rent from.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The three rental platforms averaged a <strong>D, an F, and a C</strong>.
      The Squarespace sites clustered together near the bottom, and the Wix
      sites landed within a few points of one another. Templated software tends
      to produce templated results. For comparison, the custom-coded sites I build, and the
      ones I checked alongside them, averaged <strong>94 on mobile with an A-plus
      security grade</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ins-h">What this means for your business</h2>
    <p>
      This is not a knock on any of these businesses. It is an open lane. In a
      market where almost no one has done the work, doing it well puts you ahead
      fast. A site that loads in under a second, earns a real security grade, and
      has clean SEO can outrank nearly everything around it locally, often within
      weeks, because the competition simply is not trying.
    </p>
    <p>
      You do not have to take my word for where your own site stands. The same
      checks I used are built into a free tool on this site.
      <a href="/audit" class="ins-link">Run a free audit of your site →</a>
    </p>

    <p class="ins-note">
      Methodology: 169 publicly reachable Coos County small-business websites,
      scored with Google Lighthouse (mobile) and Mozilla Observatory in 2026.
      Platform identified by site fingerprint.
    </p>
  </article>

  <Divider label="02" />

  <Contact sectionNumber="02" centered suppressHeader />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .ins { max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 16px 4px 8px; }
  .ins-meta { font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--color-copper); text-transform: uppercase; margin: 0 0 18px; }
  .ins-lead {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-ash-200, var(--color-ash-100));
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }
  .ins-lead strong, .ins p strong { color: var(--color-ash-100); font-weight: 600; }
  .ins p {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-ash-300);
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .ins-h {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 24px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ash-100);
    margin: 32px 0 12px;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  @media (min-width: 740px) { .ins-h { font-size: 28px; } }
  .ins-fig { margin: 26px 0; }
  .ins-fig img {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 520px;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-card, 10px);
    border: 1px solid var(--copper-hairline);
  }
  .ins-fig figcaption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    color: var(--color-ash-500);
    text-align: center;
    margin-top: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  }
  .ins-link {
    color: var(--color-wc-green);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: rgba(77, 216, 168, 0.4);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .ins-note {
    font-size: 13px !important;
    color: var(--color-ash-500) !important;
    font-style: italic;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
    padding-top: 16px;
    margin-top: 28px !important;
  }
</style>
